      "problem": "Consider the following triangle:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14249653/assets/e03942073ddfd8672fe5c5e4bd7bc81f0dc22f8682e3a364b001c280b315e721.png)\n\nCalculate the measure of the angle $\\angle A$ to two decimal places.",
      "worked_solution": "We use the Law of Cosines to solve for angle $A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡A=\\frac{{b}^{2}+{c}^{2}-{a}^{2}}{2bc}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have\n\n$$\na=9,\\quad b=7,\\quad c=12.\n$$\n\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡A & =\\frac{{7}^{2}+{12}^{2}-{9}^{2}}{2(7)(12)} \\\\  & =\\frac{49+144-81}{168} \\\\  & =\\frac{112}{168} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠A & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{2}{3}) \\\\  & ={48.19}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto two decimal places.",
